We can find two vectors that are both perpendicular to $\langle 1, 2, 3\rangle,$ in order to find two vectors that are both parallel to our plane. For example, we can use the vectors $\langle 2, -2, 0 \rangle$ and $\langle -3, 0, 2 \rangle$.
Now we have a parametric equation for our plane. Let $t$ and $s$ be parameters, then the plane is:
$$
\begin{align*}
\langle x, y, z \rangle &= \langle x_0, y_0, z_0\rangle + t\langle l, m, n \rangle + s\langle p, q, r\rangle \\
&= \langle x_0, y_0, z_0 \rangle + t\langle 2, -2, 0 \rangle + s\langle -3, 0, 2 \rangle \\
&= \langle -1, 1, 1 \rangle + t\langle 2, -2, 0 \rangle + s\langle -3, 0, 2 \rangle
\end{align*}
$$
Rewriting our parametric equation as a system of equations gives
$$
\begin{align*}
x &= -1 + 2t - 3s \\
y &= 1 - 2t \\
z &= 1 + 2s.
\end{align*}
$$
Now we will eliminate the parameters in order to find the cartesian equation of our plane. Solving the $y$ and $z$ equations for $t$ and $s,$ respectively, gives:
$$
\begin{align*}
t &= \frac{y-1}{2} \rightarrow \text{Eqn (1)} \\
s &= \frac{z-1}{2} \rightarrow \text{Eqn (2)}
\end{align*}
$$
Substituting $\text{Eqn (1)}$ and $ \text{Eqn (2)}$ into $\text{Eqn (1)}$ gives
$$
\begin{align*}
x &= -1 + 2\left(\frac{y-1}{2}\right) - 3\left(\frac{z-1}{2}\right) \\
x &= -1 + y-1 - \frac{3}{2}z + \frac{3}{2} \\
x &= y - \frac{3}{2}z - \frac{1}{2}.
\end{align*}
$$
Finally, we solve the equation for $1$ and get the cartesian equation:
$$
\begin{align*}
2x &= 2y - 3z - 1 \\
